# Architecture Rules

## Architectural style

Use a modular monolith in one Next.js application. Organize by domain
and keep UI, application services, integrations, and persistence
separated.

Recommended structure:

``` text
src/
  app/
    (public)/
      page.tsx
      about/page.tsx
      doctors/page.tsx
      doctors/[slug]/page.tsx
      services/page.tsx
      blog/page.tsx
      blog/[slug]/page.tsx
      book-appointment/page.tsx
    (staff-auth)/
      login/page.tsx
    admin/
      layout.tsx
      dashboard/page.tsx
      doctors/
      appointments/
      payments/
      content/
      settings/
    doctor/
      layout.tsx
      dashboard/page.tsx
      availability/
      appointments/
      profile/
    api/
      auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts
      public/
      admin/
      doctor/
      webhooks/razorpay/route.ts
  components/
    ui/
    public/
    admin/
    doctor/
    shared/
  features/
    appointments/
    availability/
    doctors/
    payments/
    content/
    reports/
    users/
  lib/
    auth/
    db/
    env/
    validation/
    utils/
  server/
    services/
    repositories/
    integrations/
      razorpay/
      cloudinary/
      brevo/
  types/

prisma/
  schema.prisma
docs/
.cursor/rules/
```

The exact tree may evolve, but preserve these boundaries.

## Request flow

UI / route handler -\> input validation -\> authentication and
authorization -\> domain service -\> repository/Prisma -\> response.

External provider flow: Domain service -\> provider adapter -\> provider
API. Keep Razorpay, Cloudinary, and Brevo SDK usage inside their
respective adapters.

## Rendering

-   Public SEO pages should use Server Components and metadata.
-   Staff dashboards should fetch sensitive data on the server.
-   Do not expose full database records to client components.
-   Use client components only for interactive controls such as
    calendars, filters, and form state.

## Route protection

-   `/admin/**` requires authenticated ADMIN.
-   `/doctor/**` requires authenticated DOCTOR.
-   Staff login is shared at `/login`; redirect after login based on
    role.
-   Public website and booking pages do not require customer login.
-   API authorization must be repeated server-side even when the page
    layout is protected.

## Configuration

Use validated environment variables in one server-only module. Keep
`.env.example` committed with empty placeholders; never commit `.env`.
